        {
          "id": "seized-engine-warning-stop-attempting-to-start",
          "title": "⚠️ Seized engine warning - stop attempting to start",
          "description": "If you turn the key and absolutely nothing happens - the engine may be seized. Seized engines cannot be repaired - only replaced.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Signs of a seized engine:"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Silence when you turn the key (no crank, no click)",
                "Starter motor engages but engine doesn't turn",
                "Engine locked solid - won't turn with a breaker bar",
                "Previous symptoms - overheating, oil light, knocking before failure"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not keep trying to start a seized engine. You'll damage the starter motor and flywheel."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "✅ DO:"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Stop attempting to start · Arrange recovery to a garage · Get a diagnostic to confirm"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "❌ DON'T:"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep trying · Jump start · Push start (will damage transmission)"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Book Emergency Recovery + Diagnostic"
            },
            {
              "type": "cards",
              "cards": [
                {
                  "title": "What happens if you ignore it",
                  "text": "The cost trap: Attempting to start a seized engine can damage the starter motor and flywheel - adding £200-£600 to the replacement cost.",
                  "highlight": "£60-£200",
                  "meta": "",
                  "icon": "",
                  "items": [],
                  "fullText": "What happens if you ignore it The cost escalates quickly Symptom Progression Cost Impact Slow crank (battery) Battery fails completely £60-£200 Starter clicks Starter motor burns out £100-£400 Cranks but won't start Fuel pump fails £150-£900 Seized engine Engine must be replaced £1,500-£8,000+ The cost trap: Attempting to start a seized engine can damage the starter motor and flywheel - adding £200-£600 to the replacement cost."
                },
                {
                  "title": "What to do next",
                  "text": "Step 1: Don't panic - listen carefully Turn the key and listen: Clicking → Battery issue. No sound → Starter or seized engine. Cranking sound → Fuel/spark/compression issue. Step 2: Check the battery Do lights come on? Are they dim? Try jump starting (if safe). If jump starting works → battery is the issue. Step 3: Call a professional (£50-£150) A technician will test battery voltage and condition, check starter motor function, check fuel pressure and spark, test compression if required, and confirm if engine is seized.",
                  "highlight": "",
                  "meta": "",
                  "icon": "",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 1: Don't panic - listen carefully Turn the key and listen: Clicking → Battery issue. No sound → Starter or seized engine. Cranking sound → Fuel/spark/compression issue.",
                    "Step 2: Check the battery Do lights come on? Are they dim? Try jump starting (if safe). If jump starting works → battery is the issue.",
                    "Step 3: Call a professional (£50-£150) A technician will test battery voltage and condition, check starter motor function, check fuel pressure and spark, test compression if required, and confirm if engine is seized."
                  ],
                  "fullText": "What to do next Don't panic - listen carefully Step 1: Don't panic - listen carefully Turn the key and listen: Clicking → Battery issue. No sound → Starter or seized engine. Cranking sound → Fuel/spark/compression issue. Step 2: Check the battery Do lights come on? Are they dim? Try jump starting (if safe). If jump starting works → battery is the issue. Step 3: Call a professional (£50-£150) A technician will test battery voltage and condition, check starter motor function, check fuel pressure and spark, test compression if required, and confirm if engine is seized. Book Mobile Diagnostic"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
